{"id":"neet-2026-q017","pn":17,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Power","q":"The power of a crane, which lifts a mass of 1000 kg to a height of 20 m in 10 s is: (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["19.6 W","39.2 W","39.2 kW","19.6 kW"],"c":3,"s":"Power = Work/Time.\nWork = mgh, so P = mgh/t.\nP = (10³ × 9.8 × 20)/10 = 19.6 × 10³ W.\nP = 19.6 kW."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q025","pn":25,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Work done by variable friction","q":"A particle of mass M moves along a horizontal x axis from x = 0 to x = L. The coefficient of kinetic friction varies as a function of x as μₖ(x) = μ₀ − αx, where μ₀, α are constants of appropriate dimensions, so that μₖ(L) = 0. The total work done by the frictional force during the motion is nμ₀MgL, where g is the acceleration due to gravity. The value of n is:","o":["1","1/3","1/2","3"],"c":2,"s":"Given μₖ(x) = μ₀ − αx and μₖ(L) = 0 = μ₀ − αL, so μ₀ = αL.\nMagnitude of work by friction: W_f = ∫dW_f.\ndW_f = fₖdx = μₖN dx = μₖMg dx = (μ₀ − αx)Mg dx.\nW_f = ∫₀ᴸ μ₀Mg dx − ∫₀ᴸ αMg x dx = μ₀Mg[x]₀ᴸ − αMg[x²/2]₀ᴸ.\nnμ₀MgL = μ₀MgL − (μ₀/L)Mg(L²/2) = μ₀MgL/2.\nSo n = 1/2."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q033","pn":33,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Elastic collision and energy conservation","q":"Bob B of mass m at rest is hanging vertically from the ceiling via a massless string of length 10 m, as shown in the figure. Point mass A of mass m travelling horizontally with speed 10 ms⁻¹ hits bob B elastically. The bob B rises h meter after the collision. Taking the acceleration due to gravity g = 10 ms⁻² and neglecting the size of the bob, the value of h is:","o":["7","5","2.5","8"],"c":1,"s":"To reach the horizontal position, the bob would need a speed of 10√2 m/s.\nIn an elastic collision of equal masses, the velocities are interchanged.\nSo the bob moves with 10 m/s after the collision.\nBy conservation of energy: mgh = (1)/(2)mv².\nh = v²/(2g).\nh = (10)²/(2 × 10).\nh = 5 m.","fig":{"w":300,"h":200,"alt":"A bob B hangs from a ceiling on a vertical string; point mass A at the same level on the left moves right at 10 ms⁻¹ toward B.","d":[["l",150,15,190,15,"B"],["l",170,15,170,150],["c",170,150,3,"F"],["t",178,148,"B","s"],["c",110,150,3,"F"],["t",106,144,"A","es"],["l",115,150,145,150,"a"],["t",128,172,"10 ms⁻¹","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q005","pn":5,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Work-energy theorem","q":"The kinetic energies of two similar cars A and B are 100 J and 225 J respectively. On applying breaks, car A stops after 1000 m and car B stops after 1500 m. If F_A and F_B are the forces applied by the breaks on cars A and B respectively, then the ratio of F_A/F_B is","o":["3/2","2/3","1/3","1/2"],"c":1,"s":"By work-energy theorem, work done by the brakes = change in K.E.\n−FS = k_f − k_i\n⇒ FS = k_i − k_f = k_i (car stops, k_f = 0)\n⇒ F_A/F_B = (k_A/k_B) × (S_B/S_A)\n= (100/225) × (1500/1000)\n= 150/225 = 2/3"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q007","pn":7,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Vertical circular motion, slack string","q":"A bob of heavy mass m is suspended by a light string of length l. The bob is given a horizontal velocity v₀ as shown in figure. If the string gets slack at some point P making an angle θ from the horizontal, the ratio of the speed v of the bob at point P to its initial speed v₀ is:","o":["(sinθ)^(1/2)","(1/(2 + 3sinθ))^(1/2)","(cosθ/(2 + 3sinθ))^(1/2)","(sinθ/(2 + 3sinθ))^(1/2)"],"c":3,"s":"At point P the string is slack, so the radial component of weight gives the centripetal force:\nmg sinθ = mv²/l ...(1)\nConservation of mechanical energy between Q (bottom) and P:\n(1/2)mv₀² = (1/2)mv² + mg(l + l sinθ)\nv₀²/2 = v²/2 + gl(1 + sinθ)\nFrom (1), gl = v²/sinθ. Put this in:\nv₀²/2 = v²/2 + (v²/sinθ)(1 + sinθ)\nv₀²/2 = v²/2 + v²/sinθ + v²\nv₀²/2 = (3/2)v² + 2v²/(2 sinθ)\nv₀² = v²[3 + 2/sinθ]\nv/v₀ = (sinθ/(3 sinθ + 2))^(1/2)","fig":{"w":190,"h":190,"alt":"A bob m at the bottom of a circle of centre O hangs from a string of length l and is given horizontal velocity v0; point P on the upper right of the circle makes angle theta with the dashed horizontal through O.","d":[["p","M88.2 8.1 A69.3 69.3 0 0 0 9 63",""],["p","M88.2 8.1 A69.3 69.3 0 1 1 9 63","d"],["l",78.3,78.3,78.3,146.7,"B"],["l",78.3,78.3,126,27,"B"],["l",78.3,78.3,150.3,78.3,"d"],["c",126,27,2.3,"F"],["c",78.3,146.7,2.7,"F"],["l",67.5,81.9,67.5,144,"b"],["t",69.3,117,"l",""],["p","M100.8 78.3 A22.5 22.5 0 0 0 93.6 62.1",""],["t",100.8,72.9,"θ",""],["t",69.3,72.9,"O","e"],["t",135,22.5,"P",""],["l",81.9,146.7,117,146.7,"a"],["t",122.4,152.1,"v₀",""],["t",78.3,165.6,"m","m"]]},"sfig":{"w":260,"h":215,"alt":"Circle with centre O, bottom point Q with velocity v0 to the right; at P up on the right the velocity v is up-left, mg points down, mg sin theta points along PO and the tangent makes 90 - theta with the horizontal.","d":[["c",79.2,96.3,69.3,""],["l",79.2,96.3,79.2,164.7,"B"],["l",79.2,96.3,126.9,46.8,"B"],["l",79.2,96.3,148.5,96.3,"d"],["r",74.7,160.2,9,9,""],["l",83.7,164.7,119.7,164.7,"a"],["t",123.3,170.1,"v₀",""],["t",78.3,187.2,"Q","m"],["c",126.9,46.8,1.8,"F"],["l",126.9,46.8,98.1,19.8,"a"],["t",86.4,18.9,"v",""],["l",126.9,46.8,126.9,72,"a"],["t",129,81,"mg","s"],["l",126.9,46.8,107.1,64.8,"a"],["t",50.4,64.8,"mgsinθ",""],["l",126.9,46.8,150.3,46.8,""],["t",155.7,50.4,"90 − θ",""],["p","M139.5 46.8 A12.6 12.6 0 0 0 135.9 37.8",""],["p","M101.7 96.3 A22.5 22.5 0 0 0 94.5 79.2",""],["t",99.9,90,"θ",""],["t",72,92.7,"O","e"],["t",131,37,"P",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q022","pn":22,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Instantaneous power","q":"At any instant of time t, the displacement of any particle is given by 2t – 1 (SI unit) under the influence of force of 5 N. The value of instantaneous power is (in SI unit) :","o":["7","6","10","5"],"c":2,"s":"x = 2t − 1.\nv = dx/dt = 2 m s⁻¹.\nP = F·v = 5 × 2 = 10 W."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q030","pn":30,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Inelastic collision","q":"Two bodies A and B of same mass undergo completely inelastic one dimensional collision. The body A moves with velocity v₁ while body B is at rest before collision. The velocity of the system after collision is v₂. The ratio v₁ : v₂ is-","o":["4 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1"],"c":3,"s":"Before collision A moves with v₁ and B is at rest.\nThe collision is completely inelastic, so they move together with v₂.\nMomentum is conserved: mv₁ = mv₂ + mv₂ = 2mv₂.\nSo v₁/v₂ = 2/1.","sfig":{"w":170,"h":70,"alt":"Before collision: ball A moves with velocity v1 towards ball B which is at rest.","d":[["c",20,25,11],["t",20,29,"A","m"],["l",31,25,66,25,"a"],["t",70,29,"v₁"],["c",120,25,11],["t",120,29,"B","m"],["t",120,52,"rest","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q025","pn":25,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Spring potential energy","q":"The potential energy of a long spring when stretched by 2 cm is U. If the spring is stretched by 8 cm, potential energy stored in it will be:","o":["16U","2U","4U","8U"],"c":0,"s":"Potential energy = ½Kx².\nAt x = 2 cm: U = ½K(2)² = 2K.\nAt x = 8 cm: U' = ½K(8)² = 32K.\nU'/U = 32K/2K = 16, so U' = 16U."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q014","pn":14,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Energy from power","q":"The energy that will be ideally radiated by a 100 kW transmitter in 1 hour is","o":["36 × 10⁴ J","36 × 10⁵ J","1 × 10⁵ J","36 × 10⁷ J"],"c":3,"s":"Energy = power × time.\nE = 100 × 10³ W × 3600 s = 36 × 10⁷ J."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q015","pn":15,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Work, Energy and Power","st":"Power of a lift","q":"An electric lift with a maximum load of 2000 kg (lift + passengers) is moving up with a constant speed of 1.5 ms⁻¹. The frictional force opposing the motion is 3000 N. The minimum power delivered by the motor to the lift in watts is : (g = 10 m s⁻²)","o":["20000","34500","23500","23000"],"c":1,"s":"Speed is constant, so the motor force balances weight plus friction.\nF = 2000 × 10 + 3000 = 23000 N.\nPower = F·v = 23000 × 1.5 = 34500 W."}
